Workspace development (@workspace-development: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @workspace) { r_4991VAVEDZ39R24CG31M8R1TZ0
Develop one native Workspace through verified shared foundations and explicit dependent feature branches. The same object retains its identity/source across views and supported behavior models. This Record replaces the serial Markdown guide; dependency edges and owned package exports determine readiness, while contract Records retain all detailed requirements and composed acceptance.

## Next action and context budget

- [ ] Complete the historical baseline's isolated resumption check before implementation depends on its measurements. This conversion itself implements no application capability and performs no runtime baseline rerun.
- [ ] Select an authorized package whose explicit prerequisites are verified. Read its start gate, ordered tasks, exported boundary, shared-service ownership and referenced contract sections; do not preload every scientific or geographic branch.
- [ ] Reserve actual file/service ownership and any required machine/benchmark window. Independent branches may work concurrently only on stable provider contracts. If uncertain about a dependency or shared mutable subsystem, serialize or add the missing edge before coding.
- [ ] Recover provider handoffs rather than redesigning their APIs. Native UI, backend, persistence, authority and required tests are part of each usable slice; a speculative interface or mock cannot unlock consumers.
- [ ] Record current source/build, exported API/revision, supported units/limits, owned tasks/workflows, proof recipe, observed results, active repairs and next action in the package's handoff. Pending fields cannot advance status.
- [ ] Full-core acceptance joins the complete core product; it is not a gate preventing unrelated verified numerical or authoring branches from being implemented. Numerical work still needs its named prerequisites, scoped implementation authorization and independent mathematical/resource evidence. This scheduling interpretation follows the owner's request for actual dependency-based parallel work and supersedes the old numerical-tail priority order.

## Workspace resumption and isolated validation

- [ ] Preserve the 2026-10-25 Stage 01 evidence as historical: the owner reports many simultaneous agents and builds during its implementation and testing. Their CPU/RAM/GPU/I/O use, build contention and concurrent source changes can affect measurements and reproduction. Stage 01's scoped delivery remains historically verified; isolated revalidation is a new prerequisite for dependent work, not proof that an earlier capability has regressed.

- [ ] Reserve the isolated resumption and future performance/whole-application acceptance windows after other development/build/test workloads have finished. Ordinary feature development may proceed concurrently outside those windows when the dependency and ownership rules permit it; normal OS services and the application's own workers remain expected. Complete required compilation before timed runtime sampling and record actual machine/build/viewport, source revision or hashes, cache conditions, instrumentation and remaining background load. Do not stop other agents or alter their work to obtain exclusivity.

- [ ] Before implementing the durable model package, inspect intervening source changes, rerun Stage 01's fixture validation and targeted correctness suites, and repeat **01.W01** through native hit/drag, queued controls, Area attraction, gravity, settling, foreign-workspace isolation and idle sampling. Review the recorded harness/setup adjustments and supported Area settings as well as the final successful replay; inspect fresh screenshots, traces and saved-state observations. Use fresh output directories and retain the original evidence unchanged.

- [ ] Revisit every [Stage 01 finding](../../../../fixtures/workspace/stage01/qualitative-review.json), including active/idle performance and CPU, renderer workspace-switch allocation diagnostics, synthetic Wayland cursor behavior, Area/selection label overlap and accessibility environment warnings. Record each as reproduced, not reproduced under the stated conditions, environment-dependent or unresolved, with its reproducer/evidence and next owner. A disappearance during one isolated run does not establish that other agents caused it; a non-timing diagnostic remains a defect candidate until investigated.

- [ ] Capture a fresh isolated performance baseline using the existing measurement definitions; separate correctness/instrumented replay from representative timing runs. Record sample counts, active/idle CPU and frame/input/resource results, and compare the historical run only with explicit configuration differences. Keep future optimized targets intact and unavailable counters explicit. Investigate any reproduced failure of an earlier required workflow at its earliest owning stage before dependent work; retain later-stage ownership for issues outside that workflow's gate.

- [ ] Close this resumption gate only after the isolated workflow pass, finding-by-finding review and fresh measured baseline are recorded in the Stage 01 handoff with evidence links and remaining owners. Update the baseline handoff and durable model package's entry check with the actual result. If a required earlier workflow fails, mark `Repair required` under the normal repair rules; until the resumption gate is satisfied, the durable model package remains Pending.

## Dependency-driven delivery and repair

- [ ] `#requires` on a work package names a hard implementation prerequisite: its verified export must exist before dependent coding starts. `#requires` on a contract acceptance Record names the contributors needed for the whole composed proof. A body link is a context/reference relationship, not an extra scheduling edge. No numbered-stage adjacency creates a dependency.
- [ ] Within each package execute its ordered tasks and integration cycles sequentially. Across packages use the graph. Independent mathematical methods are not an automatic permission to edit the same service concurrently; actual module ownership, working-tree isolation and resource admission must also permit overlap.
- [ ] A prerequisite export contains concrete schemas/units/source identity, accepted command/query/save paths, authority/revision semantics, error/cancellation/state lifetime, measured budgets and a real native fixture. Stabilize shared source, view, selection, asset, relation and runtime services with their first real consumer before several branches extend them.
- [ ] One owner changes each shared model schema, store migration, command protocol, query projection, asset lifecycle, renderer frame/input path or runtime scheduler at a time. Others consume the published API or queue a reviewed extension. There is no parallel duplicate core later to merge. Consumers use independently owned leaf adapters and native modules; inspect actual code boundaries before starting, because a plan cannot guarantee conflict-free files.
- [ ] Independent development and untimed local checks may overlap. Reserve exclusive windows for native global-state acceptance, migration/integration checks requiring shared resources, and representative performance sampling. Compile before timed sampling; document residual OS load. The earlier owner request for isolated baseline measurement is retained, not expanded into a ban on all parallel development.
- [ ] Each feature closes its own complete bounded scope. A later integration package composes already proven exports; it cannot retroactively call an incomplete provider done. A known later domain extension stays a separate pending package. Retain native usability, offline/source/rights requirements and appropriate tests at each boundary instead of leaving all correctness to a final merge.
- [ ] Avoid unnecessary reopening by freezing verified exports, tracking source generations and testing required consumers at boundary changes. This cannot promise zero revisits: a discovered defect or changed requirement needs a named owner, affected workflows, targeted repair and revalidation. Mark only affected verification invalid; unrelated verified branches and unchanged evidence remain useful. Never carry an unresolved required regression into a completion claim.
- [ ] Complete a composed contract only after every contributing package and its actual multi-feature replay pass. A branch may publish its narrower verified export earlier. The core acceptance join proves the entire requested core experience; each scientific claim retains a separate reference/validity gate. Empty extension slots and successful compilation are not feature completion.

## Workflow contracts and balanced acceptance

- [ ] Treat this guide as context routing and each stage as the detailed specification for its pending work. Retain architecture, small consequential decisions, ownership, data structures, schemas/interfaces, algorithms, stack constraints, examples and failure paths at the owning task. Distinguish an illustrative sketch from a selected production interface and record how unresolved choices will be decided. Detail has no arbitrary word cap while it prevents a real implementation ambiguity; do not copy every past or future subsystem into the current context.

- [ ] Keep the stage's opening milestones and `NN.Wxx` rows as persistent capability contracts. Classify each workflow as a **built-in capability** delivered by that stage, an **example recipe** composing existing primitives with a small saved arrangement/asset/configuration, or a named combination of both. State any adapter actually required. A recipe uses the shared edit/save/query/authority/demand path; a new independent product implementation requires an explicit scope decision.

- [ ] Preserve every required example as evidence while keeping its data outside generic engine logic. The solar/ball journey, invented scene, room/castle, CAD bracket, articulated body/mechanism and laboratory specimen prove supported operations and composition; their names, dimensions, appearance and geographic hierarchy do not become mandatory engine categories. An optional idea does not silently become a delivery gate.

- [ ] For every retained workflow, capture fixture/source/build/profile, starting state, native actions, expected observable result, required primitive combination, hard assertions, qualitative criteria, quantitative thresholds and failure/recovery cases. Map every required milestone to at least one row; a short matrix row points to detailed owned requirements instead of weakening them. In completed stages retain a separate compact paragraph for each workflow, so earlier intended uses remain recoverable.

- [ ] Separate **hard requirements** from **soft quality criteria**, and separate both from measurements still being explored. Hard requirements include supported operations, identity/units/authority, integrity/recovery and adopted numeric error/resource/latency bounds. Soft criteria include low-poly appeal, readable content, predictable controls, useful feedback and understandable repair. Give soft criteria a task-specific rubric, views/actions and recorded pass/fail observations; they remain acceptance obligations rather than optional polish. A reference screenshot supports review but does not replace interaction or structural assertions.

- [ ] Adopt actual workload sizes, thresholds, measurement points and hardware from stage 01 and the owning fixture/profile before implementing the tested slice. Proposed numbers stay identified until selected; unspecified values remain pending with an owner. Record absolute results and applicable baseline deltas, including transient peaks, cold/warm/offline conditions, percentile sampling and relevant accuracy. A faster result with weakened detail, precision, coverage or permissions is a changed experiment. Change a governing requirement/profile only through the existing explicit scope-review process; ordinary implementation choices reuse existing authorization.

- [ ] Combine meaningful test levels: unit/property/metamorphic checks for local invariants; integration and controlled fault/retry tests for accepted operations, persistence, jobs and authority; native end-to-end interaction simulation for the real widgets/input/focus/command path; and bounded performance/resource runs plus qualitative review of the actual experience. Choose levels that expose the changed risk. Stage 01 validates planning/baseline artifacts, and future solvers run only in their authorized stages. A rendered square count, a compiled example or a mocked service alone cannot complete a composed workflow.

- [ ] Drive native interaction simulations through supported pointer/keyboard/tool inputs and the actual services, then assert selected identities, accepted revisions/receipts, visible state and saved/reopened results as applicable. Use controlled clocks/completions for races and keep timing benchmarks separate from instrumentation-heavy tests. Record unsupported automation and a reproducible manual interaction recipe where necessary; a skipped required interaction keeps its evidence pending. Keep tests proportional to the feature and failure boundary.

- [ ] For each newly composed experience, retain at least one relevant already verified user workflow and identify the earlier contracts that could break. Test interoperability through the actual public/module boundary rather than requiring the same internal implementation. Stable capabilities may survive a justified rewrite; tests should not freeze incidental helper names, private struct layout or arbitrary screenshot pixels.

- [ ] Keep a traceable chain for each required outcome: `NN.Mxx → NN.Wxx → owning NN.Txx/requirement → acceptance replay → observed evidence → retained handoff`. The stage-local `NN.Pxx` proof recipes below make this chain concrete; use them within the written task order, not as a competing implementation sequence. One proof can cover several requirements, but explicitly list its coverage. A requirement without evidence stays open; an example recipe cannot conceal an unimplemented primitive or adapter.

- [ ] Before the affected implementation starts, fill each proof recipe's workload dimensions, adopted limits and observables from the named fixture/profile. Separate input size, active set and addressable extent; separate unique source assets from occurrences, participants and views. Name the unit, sampling interval/count, start/stop event and pass condition for each measurement. Unknown, unsupported, not measured, failed and passed are different states; an empty field or an unavailable GPU counter is never zero or a pass.

- [ ] Make checks capable of detecting a plausible defect: derive expected quantities/identities from an independently specified fixture, reference calculation or invariant rather than echoing the implementation's output. Use controlled completions/clocks for reproducible acceptance races, and real elapsed time for performance. Record seeds/failure points where relevant. A single interaction sample is a sample, not a meaningful p95; select enough samples and report their count without making ordinary correctness tests depend on benchmark timing.

- [ ] Define qualitative pass observations before inspecting the result: which actions/viewpoints expose the concern, what the user must distinguish or accomplish, and which visible/control failures require repair. Record the actual observation/reviewer and native evidence. Automation may establish interaction/focus/identity invariants; an unreviewed screenshot cannot establish attractive low-poly form, comprehensible repair or useful character motion.

- [ ] Instantiate this illustrative acceptance card for the selected workflow using the stage's real IDs and thresholds; it is planning/evidence metadata, not a new application API or a default requirement to build a test framework:

  ```yaml
  workflow_id: NN.W01
  milestones: [NN.M01]
  delivery_kind: built-in-or-example-recipe
  primitive_contracts: []
  earlier_workflows_at_risk: []
  fixture_source_build_profile: pending
  initial_state_and_native_actions: []
  hard_invariants_and_numeric_bounds: []
  quality_rubric_and_observations: []
  unit_integration_fault_native_checks: []
  performance_recipe_and_observed_results: pending
  evidence_and_repair_links: []
  state: pending
  ```

- [ ] Extend that existing card only with the selected proof's needed fields; this illustrative result shape organizes evidence and does not authorize a new test framework, database or application schema:

  ```yaml
  proof_id: NN.P01
  covers: [NN.M01, NN.W01, NN.T01]
  fixture_profile_and_build: pending
  workload:
    total_input: pending
    active_subset: pending
    views_and_participants: pending
  quantitative_checks:
    - metric: pending
      unit_and_measurement_boundary: pending
      adopted_limit_and_source: pending
      samples_and_observed_result: pending
      evidence: pending
      state: not-measured
  qualitative_checks:
    - action_or_view: pending
      pass_observation: pending
      actual_observation_and_reviewer: pending
      state: not-reviewed
  retained_earlier_workflows: []
  faults_and_recovery_evidence: []
  next_action_or_repair: pending
  ```

- [ ] Use the following measurement boundaries where applicable; the owning profile supplies limits and workload sizes. Record unsupported instrumentation honestly instead of substituting another quantity with the same label.

  | Measurement | Required boundary and interpretation |
  | --- | --- |
  | Input response | Supported native input event to first observed corresponding visible update; name whether presentation/scanout is actually observed. Exclude model-provider waiting from native response and report it separately. |
  | Accepted edit | Request submission to authoritative acceptance/receipt, plus a separate visible-final-result time when derivation continues. A preview is not an accepted commit. |
  | Frames and work | Identify actual frame interval, CPU update duration and GPU duration separately; report sample count/window and settings. Fixed simulation steps or CPU update timers alone do not measure display frame latency. |
  | Opening and coverage | Start at the declared local/host open event; stop at a useful coarse, interactive view, then separately at requested refinement readiness. Specify application-cache, OS-cache and network/offline conditions without silently changing the experiment. |
  | Memory and transfer | Record managed live bytes, total process/available GPU measurements, staging peaks and encoded versus decoded/in-flight bytes separately. Shared assets/views count once under aggregate admission; estimates and process RSS cannot prove exact GPU residency. |
  | Cancellation and release | Record acknowledgement, obsolete dispatch stop, worker yield/termination and final lease/fence release as separate events. Keep durable source/offline packs distinct from evictable disk cache and decoded RAM/GPU products. |
  | Accuracy | Name physical quantity/unit, source/reference, absolute/relative error and covered regime. Separate authored exactness, numerical tolerance, display error and empirical source uncertainty. |

## Completion compression and retained capability memory

- [ ] After a slice is implemented and verified, replace only its completed planning detail with factual implementation context. Summarize the selected architecture, actual module/interface ownership and why it meets the requirements in one or two short implementation paragraphs; mention a significant rejected alternative in a short sentence only when it explains a tradeoff or prevents repeating the investigation.

- [ ] Condense verified slices even while their stage is in progress, identifying the precise delivered subset and its evidence. Keep the overall workflow/stage pending until all of its required parts work together; a completed subtask or specialist calculation cannot imply completion of its surrounding workflow or every remaining domain.

- [ ] Preserve one distinct compact paragraph per retained workflow, keyed by `NN.Wxx` and its original milestone IDs. State what the user can still do, which primitives/short recipe provide it, the relevant hard bounds and quality criteria, and links to runnable evidence. The implementation paragraphs' length target does not justify dropping a workflow or its governing conditions.

- [ ] Keep authoritative schemas/contracts, fixture definitions and consequential decisions accessible at stable links in code, tests, the handoff or a compact retained section. Redirect incoming links before removing obsolete planning detail. Delete superseded pseudocode, duplicate research and completed step-by-step instructions once the real implementation/evidence takes their place; keep every unfinished, blocked and deferred requirement detailed.

- [ ] Preserve measured profile/version/source and proof links separately from instructions. The completion handoff records implementation rationale, concise relevant alternatives, accepted interfaces/ownership, workflow evidence, hard/soft results, unresolved scope, current regressions and what the next stage needs. A completed paragraph is a capability contract with traceable evidence, not a success story.

- [ ] When an earlier contract changes, update its retained workflow paragraphs and factual handoff to the new verified implementation without erasing the original capability requirement. Repair affected later references/tests and return to the next ready owned task. A future agent should recover the next task from the guide, required handoffs and selected package without reconstructing the conversation.

- [ ] Prune by verified requirement rather than by checked line count. If a paragraph mixes delivered and pending work, split it and retain the unfinished portion with its original ID/owner. Preserve links/anchors used by later stages; keep an alias or update every incoming reference before removing a heading. Never shorten a requirement by deleting its limits, failure behavior, native interaction or workflow composition.

- [ ] Make each retained workflow paragraph answer: **what still works; built-in capability or short recipe; which source/command/representation boundaries implement it; hard limits and quality obligations; where to rerun its proof**. Keep implementation/rationale to one or two paragraphs overall and useful rejected alternatives to a sentence; keep separate workflow paragraphs even when the stage has many. Leave full pending architecture, schemas, algorithms and examples available until their work is verified.

- [ ] End each working cycle with the next ready owned task, delivered subset, decisive result and next action/repair in the existing card. Do not create another plan or re-investigate settled alternatives merely because context was lost. Repeated failure warrants a changed hypothesis or targeted architecture decision; repeating a passing unchanged check warrants no new completion claim.

